   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-precalculus",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.9",
    "topic_title": "Rational Functions and Vertical Asymptotes",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = (x²−9)/(x²−x−6). Which statement identifies all vertical asymptotes of the graph of f?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The vertical asymptotes are x = −2 and x = 3.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The denominator-zero trap treats every zero of the original denominator as a vertical asymptote. The factor x−3 cancels, so the outputs do not become unbounded near x = 3. Fix: Cancel common factors before finding zeros of the remaining denominator."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The only vertical asymptote is x = 3.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The canceled-factor trap selects the zero of the shared factor instead of the surviving denominator factor. After cancellation, x+2 remains in the denominator, while x−3 does not. Fix: Set the remaining denominator, not the canceled factor, equal to zero."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The only vertical asymptote is x = 2.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The sign-error trap solves x+2 = 0 as x = 2. Substituting 2 gives a denominator of 4 in the simplified expression, not zero. Fix: Solve x+2 = 0 by subtracting 2 from both sides."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The only vertical asymptote is x = −2.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Factoring gives f(x) = [(x−3)(x+3)]/[(x−3)(x+2)], which simplifies to (x+3)/(x+2) for inputs in the original domain. Near x = −2, the numerator approaches 1 while the denominator approaches zero, so the outputs become unbounded; cancellation prevents unbounded behavior near x = 3."
     }
    ]
   },
